1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 Bg4 $2 4. dxe5 {The whole position is pointing at e2
— every signpost reads the same: roll the central majority through it and pry
open lines toward the king there.} 4... Bxf3 {White's queen on d1 is crying out
for help that its defenders cannot give. It is attacked by Black's bishop on f3
but defended only by White's king on e1. A capture sequence here wins roughly a
queen. Meanwhile, Black's bishop on f3 hangs — it stands under attack with no
friend in sight. It is under attack from White's queen on d1 and the pawn on
g2.} 5. Qxf3 dxe5 6. Bc4 Nf6 7. Qb3 Qe7 8. Nc3 c6 {White's minor pieces have
found real work; their counterparts are still waiting for a career. White owns
the bishop pair in an open position. Here is what White should be dreaming
about: open the position and let the bishops finally stretch their diagonals.
Another dream for White: the enemy knight is homeless — keep every stable
square swept out from under it (key squares: f6 and b8).} 9. Bg5 b5 $4 {White's
bishop on c4 has collected more enemies than friends. The attack from Black's
pawn on b5 is met only by White's queen on b3. If the captures start, the
attacker comes out ahead by about two pawns. Meanwhile, White's queen on b3 has
nowhere to run — the cage is built, and the door is about to close. Only
White's pawn on a2, the pawn on c2 and the bishop on c4 keep it defended.} 10.
Nxb5 {White's knight on b5 is calling for reinforcements — the defense around
it is stretched thin. It is attacked by Black's pawn on c6 but defended only by
White's queen on b3 and the bishop on c4. A capture sequence here wins about two
pawns.} 10... cxb5 11. Bxb5+ {White's pawn structure is the happier one — the
opponent's carries scars that will not heal by themselves. White has a passed
pawn on c2. Black's a7-pawn is isolated. White has a queenside pawn majority.
Here is what White should be dreaming about: march the queenside majority —
those pawns outnumber their opposition and they know it.} 11... Nbd7 {Black's
knight on d7 is trapped — the cage is shut, and there is no safe square left.
It is attacked by White's bishop on b5 but defended only by Black's knight on
f6, the queen on e7 and the king on e8.} 12. O-O-O {White's king is feeling a
draft around c1 — the shelter is thinner than it would like. The d-file in
front of the king is wide open.} 12... Rd8 13. Rxd7 {Black's queen on e7 is
crying out for help that its defenders cannot give. It is attacked by White's
rook on d7 but defended only by Black's king on e8 and the bishop on f8. A
capture sequence here wins roughly a rook. Meanwhile, White's rook on d7 is
crying out for help that its defenders cannot give. It is attacked by Black's
knight on f6, the queen on e7, the rook on d8 and the king on e8 but defended
only by White's bishop on b5. White's heavy pieces have the open lines to
themselves. The d-file is open. The b- and c-files are half-open for Black.
White has a rook on the seventh rank. Meanwhile, White is asking the questions;
the opponent is stuck finding answers. Here is what White should be dreaming
about: the rook dreams of the seventh rank, where every enemy pawn lives at its
mercy (key squares: d7). Another dream for White: the enemy king is feeling the
draft — open a line and let the heavy pieces through (key squares: d7).
Another dream for White: double the heavy pieces on the open file and make it
their private highway.} 13... Rxd7 {Black is ahead on material. Black is three
pawns up.} 14. Rd1 Qe6 15. Bxd7+ {The defenders of Black's queen on e6 are
overworked, and they know it. The attack from White's queen on b3 and the bishop
on d7 is met only by Black's pawn on f7. If the captures start, the attacker
comes out ahead by roughly a queen. Meanwhile, Black's queen on e6 is trapped
— the cage is shut, and there is no safe square left.} 15... Nxd7 16. Qb8+
{White's queen on b8 hangs — it stands under attack with no friend in sight.
It is under attack from Black's knight on d7.} 16... Nxb8 {Black has won enough
material to decide the game; the rest is care and technique. Black is up a
